<%# locals: (room:) %>
<%# The click-to-load Pannellum stage. The pre-load image is the flat
    rectilinear render (Room#flat_panorama) — the old squashed-equirect
    :poster variant only engages as a fallback (see the comment block below).
    Explicit opt-in load button, lazy `import("pannellum")`.
    `data-turbo-permanent` + id keep the booted WebGL viewer alive across
    morphs (panel review, Wathan). pannellum.css loads only here via
    content_for(:head). %>
<% content_for(:head) do %>
  <%= stylesheet_link_tag "pannellum", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
<% end %>
<%# The flat rectilinear render (Panorama::Rectilinear) IS the initial viewer
    frame — same camera, so clicking Load produces no visual jump. Rooms whose
    render has not landed (or whose source is not equirectangular) fall back to
    the old squashed-equirect :poster; without that fallback every room page
    breaks between deploy and backfill. The fallback disappears as soon as
    RenderFlatPanoramaJob finishes.

    Pannellum's `preview:` gets the same URL deliberately: it is painted as a
    flat `background-size: cover` layer (.pnlm-preview-img in pannellum.css),
    NOT mapped onto the sphere, so a rectilinear image is correct there.

    data-panorama-preview-source exists so "why does this room look wrong?" is a
    right-click, not a console session. FOUR states, because the two obvious
    ones each hide a distinct failure:

      flat_render            correct, and matches the booted viewer exactly
      flat_render_stale      a render exists but was made under a DIFFERENT
                             recipe (someone changed HFOV_DEG/ASPECT/width and
                             did not re-run the backfill) or its file is gone.
                             Still served — a stale frame beats a squashed
                             strip — but the image WILL jump on Load. Fix with
                             `bin/rails panoramas:render_flat`.
      poster_fallback_failed no render, and the source carries a permanent
                             failure tombstone — read
                             room.panorama.blob.metadata["flat_render_error"];
                             almost always a non-2:1 photo in the panorama slot.
      poster_fallback        no render yet. Normal between deploy and backfill.

    Without the split, "renders under the old recipe" reported itself as
    correct, and "will never render" was indistinguishable from "has not
    rendered yet" — the doc's own next step for both was a Rails console.

    Cost: Room#flat_render_current? ends in a blob.service.exist? probe, so this
    is one File.exist? per room-page render against the :local disk service on
    the same host. Cheap, and the thing it catches (a stamped blob whose file a
    deploy-time SIGKILL never uploaded) is otherwise a 404 image served
    forever. Revisit if this ever moves to a remote service, where the probe
    becomes a network round trip. %>
<% flat_render_attached = room.flat_panorama.attached? %>
<% preview_source = if flat_render_attached
                      room.flat_render_current? ? "flat_render" : "flat_render_stale"
                    elsif room.flat_render_failed?
                      "poster_fallback_failed"
                    else
                      "poster_fallback"
                    end %>
<%# rails_storage_proxy_path, not rails_blob_path: the redirect route sends the
    browser to the DiskController on this same Puma (single host, :local
    service, no CDN), so every pre-load image costs two round trips — and the
    signed redirect URL expires in 5 minutes, so it recurs rather than being a
    one-time cost. The proxy controller wraps send_blob_stream in
    `http_cache_forever public: true`. Caching a room's panorama frame
    IMMUTABLY is safe here precisely because a re-render never mutates a blob
    in place: RenderFlatPanoramaJob attaches a NEW blob with a NEW key, so the
    URL itself changes and no cached response can go stale.
    The :poster fallback stays on rails_representation_path — a variant is
    resolved through its own representation route, not this one. %>
<% panorama_preview_path = if flat_render_attached
                             rails_storage_proxy_path(room.flat_panorama)
                           else
                             rails_representation_path(room.panorama.variant(:poster))
                           end %>
<%# The derived alt claims a full 360-degree panorama. That is TRUE of the
    squashed-equirect :poster (which really does show the whole sweep) and
    FALSE of the flat render (roughly longitude -50..+50, about 28% of it), so
    on this branch's PRIMARY path the old string overclaims. An AUTHORED alt
    passes through untouched on purpose: a curator's description of what is in
    the room is more useful to a screen reader user than a technically accurate
    description of a 100-degree crop. %>
<% panorama_alt = if flat_render_attached && room.alt_status_for(:panorama) != :authored
                    t("media.derived_alt.panorama_flat", room: room.display_name)
                  else
                    room.alt_for(:panorama)
                  end %>
<% pano_instructions_id = "panorama_instructions_#{room.id}" %>
<%= ui :aspect_ratio, ratio: Panorama::Rectilinear::ASPECT,
      id: "room_panorama_stage",
      class: "relative rounded-md bg-surface-sunken overflow-hidden",
      data: {
        turbo_permanent: "",
        controller: "panorama",
        panorama_url_value: rails_representation_path(room.panorama.variant(:texture)),
        panorama_preview_url_value: panorama_preview_path,
        panorama_label_value: t("rooms.show.panorama_label", room: room.display_name),
        panorama_preview_source: preview_source,
        panorama_hfov_value: Panorama::Rectilinear::HFOV_DEG,
      } do %>
  <%= image_tag panorama_preview_path, alt: panorama_alt, loading: :eager,
        class: "absolute inset-0 size-full object-cover" %>
  <%# Focus indicator comes from .pnlm-container:focus-visible in
      application.css (INSET — the stage's overflow-hidden clips the app's
      standard outward focus-ring). %>
  <div data-panorama-target="viewer" hidden class="absolute inset-0"></div>
  <%# Keyboard instructions for the BOOTED viewer, and deliberately a sibling of
      the overlay rather than a child of it: the controller hides the whole
      overlay (with rooms.show.panorama_hint inside it) one line before handing
      focus to the viewer, which carries role="application" and therefore
      suppresses browse mode. Left in the overlay, the instructions are
      destroyed at the exact moment they become relevant — the user gives up
      arrow keys and virtual navigation with nothing on screen saying what they
      bought or how to leave. The controller wires this id as the viewer's
      aria-describedby on load. The Load button's own aria-describedby tooltip
      is a different message for a different (pre-load) state; both exist. %>
  <p id="<%= pano_instructions_id %>" data-panorama-target="instructions" class="sr-only">
    <%= t("rooms.show.panorama_instructions") %>
  </p>
  <%# The whole overlay is a target: it paints OVER the viewer, so the
      controller must hide ALL of it on load or it eats every drag/zoom. %>
  <div data-panorama-target="overlay" class="absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-center">
    <%# The Load button sits over an ARBITRARY poster photo — contrast against a
        raster is unknowable (and axe skips it), so it gets a fully opaque
        surface-overlay plate for deterministic AAA text/border contrast. The
        button carries its OWN hint (2026-07-14): the button is already the
        focusable trigger, so we follow UI::Tooltip's prescribed
        interactive-control pattern — aria-describedby + the bubble reused via
        `bubble_classes`, NOT the component's own tabbable wrapper (which would
        add a nested second tab stop next to the button). Hover or focus the
        button → bubble; Esc dismisses via the shared floating controller
        (WCAG 1.4.13). This replaces the old standalone info chip. %>
    <% pano_tip_id = "tip_load_panorama_#{room.id}" %>
    <div class="group/tooltip relative inline-flex min-h-11 items-center"
         style="anchor-name: --<%= pano_tip_id %>"
         data-controller="floating"
         data-action="keydown.esc->floating#dismiss mouseleave->floating#clearDismissed focusout->floating#clearDismissed">
      <%= ui :button, t("rooms.show.load_panorama"), variant: :outline, tone: :neutral,
            class: "bg-surface-overlay shadow-sm",
            aria: { describedby: pano_tip_id },
            data: { action: "panorama#load" } %>
      <span id="<%= pano_tip_id %>" role="tooltip" style="position-anchor: --<%= pano_tip_id %>"
            class="<%= UI::TooltipComponent.bubble_classes(side: :top) %>">
        <%= t("rooms.show.panorama_hint") %>
      </span>
    </div>
  </div>
<% end %>
